    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1075,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1091,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152614,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours occur in the ovary.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:18:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152615,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients typically present with symptoms attributable to an ovarian mass, or the tumour may be incidentally discovered on imaging. Hormonal manifestations are usually absent.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:18:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152616,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Signet ring stromal tumour is exceedingly rare, with cases reported to occur at a mean age of 49&nbsp;years (range: 21&ndash;83&nbsp;years) {7483017; 14668550; 20664453; 30676431; 36040033}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:18:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152617,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:18:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152618,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:18:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152619,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Signet ring stromal tumour is usually unilateral, but occasional bilateral tumours have been reported {30676431; 20664453}. They have a mean size of 130&nbsp;mm (range: 30&ndash;200&nbsp;mm), with a solid tan-white cut surface {36040033}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:18:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152620,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Variable quantities of signet-ring cells are set in a fibromatous stroma, which resembles fibroma or cellular fibroma {7483017; 14668550}. Signet-ring cells have a single large cytoplasmic vacuole that does not contain mucin, glycogen, or lipid. Nuclei are eccentrically located, small, uniform, and bland. Small nucleoli and occasional nuclear grooves may be present. Mitoses are rare or absent. Cells with distinctive eosinophilic hyaline globules may be seen; ultrastructural studies of these globules suggest a lysosomal or erythrocytic origin {18807143; 7483017}.</p>\r\n<p>It has been suggested that most tumours with signet-ring cells with &beta;-catenin and cyclin&nbsp;D1 nuclear positivity are better classified as microcystic stromal tumour; thus, the immunohistochemical profile of signet ring stromal tumour is not well established {36040033}. Based on limited data, most are positive for SF1, CD10, and WT1, and variably express inhibin, calretinin, and cytokeratins. EMA is consistently negative {36040033}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:18:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152621,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T10:18:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
